G-Player downloads, but does not include, these third-party components at run time, each from its
original source and checked against a pinned SHA-256 (see docs/LEGAL.md):
  - Wine Staging for macOS (LGPL-2.1+), built by Gcenx: https://github.com/Gcenx/macOS_Wine_builds
  - DXVK for macOS (zlib), by Gcenx: https://github.com/Gcenx/DXVK-macOS
  - winetricks 20260125 (LGPL-2.1+): https://github.com/Winetricks/winetricks. G-Player runs it as a separate
    program and, on the user's Mac, writes a copy that refuses unverified downloads.
  - Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable (Microsoft license), from Microsoft's servers.
  - d3dcompiler_47.dll (Microsoft), as winetricks gets it: https://github.com/mozilla/fxc2
  - Other Windows components through winetricks (DirectX, .NET, PhysX, XNA, OpenAL, fonts), each under its
    maker's license, from the sources winetricks pins.
  - Store installers (Steam, Epic Games Launcher, GOG Galaxy, Ubisoft Connect) from their publishers.
  - Steam cover art from Valve's CDN, shown in the library and cached on the user's Mac.

Apple's Game Porting Toolkit and D3DMetal are never part of G-Player and are never downloaded by it.
G-Player can use a copy the user installed themselves, only when they turn it on in Settings.

